# Chapter 2 · The Second Wave (1960s–1980s): Work, Family, Body and Sexuality

> Women had the vote, yet they were still paid less, kept at home, harassed at work and denied control over their bodies. The second wave took feminism from the ballot box into the **office, the kitchen and the bedroom**. Its slogan — "**the personal is political**" — was the key to a 2026 MCQ.

## 2.1  Why a second wave?

By the 1950s women in the West could vote, but:

- most **professions** and senior jobs were closed or hostile to them;
- women were paid far less for similar work, and job adverts were divided into "men" and "women";
- the "**feminine mystique**" said women should find fulfilment as housewives;
- **contraception** was restricted and abortion illegal in most places;
- **rape in marriage**, domestic violence and sexual harassment were not treated as crimes.

Several changes prepared the ground: women's work in the **Second World War**; rising numbers of women at university; the contraceptive **pill** (approved in the USA in ==1960==); the **civil-rights movement**, which taught tactics; and **Simone de Beauvoir**'s *The Second Sex* (==1949==).

## 2.2  The two streams

The second wave had two main streams, which often disagreed (Topic 2 teaches their theories).

::: fig compare | The two streams of the second wave.
## Liberal (equal-rights) stream
- Organisations: NOW (1966)
- Leader: Betty Friedan
- Goals: equal pay, anti-discrimination laws, ERA
- Methods: lobbying, lawsuits, elections
## Women's liberation stream
- Groups: New York Radical Women, Redstockings
- Thinkers: Millett, Firestone, Hanisch
- Goals: end patriarchy in family, sex and body
- Methods: consciousness-raising, direct action
:::

::: thinker Betty Friedan | *The Feminine Mystique* (1963)
Friedan described "**the problem that has no name**" — the emptiness felt by educated suburban housewives — and co-founded the **National Organization for Women** (==1966==).
-> The book that launched the liberal stream of the second wave.
:::

## 2.3  Key events

::: fig timeline | The second wave in the United States and Britain.
@layout vertical
1961: President Kennedy's Commission on the Status of Women (chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt)
1963: Friedan's The Feminine Mystique; US Equal Pay Act
1964: US Civil Rights Act — Title VII bans sex discrimination in jobs
1966: NOW founded
1967: UK Abortion Act
1968: Miss America protest; Ford Dagenham strike by women machinists (UK)
1970: UK Equal Pay Act; Millett's Sexual Politics
1972: US Congress passes the Equal Rights Amendment (later not ratified); Title IX (education)
1973: Roe v. Wade
1975: UK Sex Discrimination Act
1979: CEDAW adopted by the UN
:::

::: case Roe v. Wade (22 January 1973) | the landmark of reproductive rights
The US Supreme Court held that the constitutional right to privacy covers a woman's decision to have an abortion, within limits. (A 2025 MCQ asked for this case.) In ==2022== the Court overturned it in ***Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization***, returning the question to the states — a sign that second-wave gains can be reversed.
:::

## 2.3a  The second wave beyond the United States

- **Britain.** The first **National Women's Liberation Conference** at Ruskin College, Oxford (==1970==), adopted four demands: **equal pay**; **equal education and opportunity**; **24-hour nurseries**; and **free contraception and abortion on demand**. The women machinists' strike at **Ford Dagenham** (==1968==) led to the **Equal Pay Act 1970**.
- **France.** In the "**Manifesto of the 343**" (==1971==), 343 women, including Simone de Beauvoir, declared publicly that they had had illegal abortions; France legalised abortion in ==1975==.
- **Iceland.** On ==24 October 1975== about **90%** of Icelandic women stopped work for a day — cooking, childcare and paid jobs — to show their value. In ==1980== Iceland elected **Vigdís Finnbogadóttir**, the world's first democratically elected woman president.
- **The global South.** Women in India, Latin America and Africa organised against dowry deaths, rape in custody, and military rule — often linking gender to class and nation.

## 2.3b  The voices of the second wave

- **Gloria Steinem** co-founded ***Ms.*** magazine (==1972==), which brought feminist ideas to a mass readership.
- **Germaine Greer** (*The Female Eunuch*, ==1970==) argued that women had been cut off from their own energy and sexuality.
- **Kate Millett** and **Shulamith Firestone** (both ==1970==) gave the radical stream its theory.
- **Angela Davis** (*Women, Race and Class*, ==1981==) linked sexism to racism and capitalism.
- **Audre Lorde** and **bell hooks** challenged the movement from within to include Black women.

## 2.4  The main issues

1. **Work and pay.** Equal pay laws; opening of professions; the end of "help wanted — male/female" adverts.
2. **Reproductive rights.** Contraception and legal abortion.
3. **Violence.** **Rape crisis centres** and **women's shelters** (the first modern shelter opened in Chiswick, London, in ==1971==); campaigns against domestic violence.
4. **Sexual harassment.** **Catharine MacKinnon** (==1979==) argued it is sex discrimination; courts later agreed.
5. **The family and housework.** Unpaid domestic work, the double burden, childcare — the "**personal is political**" (Carol Hanisch, ==1970==).
6. **Education.** Title IX (==1972==) in the USA; the rise of **Women's Studies** (Cornell ==1969==, San Diego ==1970==).

## 2.4a  What the second wave achieved, issue by issue

::: table | The second wave's main achievements.
@widths 3.2,10.8
Issue | Achievement
Pay and jobs | Equal Pay Acts (US 1963; UK 1970); Title VII (1964); UK Sex Discrimination Act (1975); professions opened
Reproduction | The pill (1960); *Griswold v. Connecticut* (1965) on contraception; UK Abortion Act (1967); *Roe v. Wade* (1973)
Violence | Rape crisis centres and shelters (from 1971); domestic violence and marital rape gradually criminalised
Harassment | Sexual harassment recognised as discrimination (MacKinnon 1979; US courts in the 1980s)
Education | Title IX (1972); Women's Studies programmes
Global | UN International Women's Year (1975); CEDAW (1979)
:::

## 2.5  Principles, goals and strategies

::: table | The second wave in one table.
@widths 3.2,10.8
Aspect | Second wave
Principles | Legal equality is not enough; inequality lives in **work, family, sexuality and culture**; "the personal is political"
Goals | Equal pay and jobs; reproductive rights; ending violence and harassment; sharing housework; changing culture
Strategies | Lobbying and lawsuits (NOW); consciousness-raising; marches and sit-ins; shelters; Women's Studies
Leaders | Friedan, Steinem, Millett, Firestone, Hanisch, MacKinnon
Main achievement | Equal-pay and anti-discrimination laws; reproductive rights; violence against women made a public issue
:::

::: define The personal is political | the second wave's slogan
Problems that seem private — housework, marriage, sex, abortion, appearance — are shaped by **power** and can be changed by **collective political action**.
-> A 2026 MCQ asked which wave this slogan is most associated with: the **second wave**.
:::

## 2.5a  Why the second wave faded

By the early 1980s the second wave had lost much of its momentum. Several reasons are usually given:

1. **Success.** Many legal goals — equal pay laws, anti-discrimination laws, legal abortion in many countries — had been won, so the movement lost its most visible targets.
2. **Division.** Liberal and radical feminists disagreed about strategy; Black and lesbian feminists felt excluded; the "**sex wars**" of the 1980s split feminists over pornography.
3. **Conservative politics.** The governments of **Ronald Reagan** (USA) and **Margaret Thatcher** (Britain) cut welfare spending and promoted traditional family values; the **Equal Rights Amendment** failed to be ratified by its ==1982== deadline.
4. **Institutionalisation.** Many activists moved into universities, government and NGOs — feminism continued, but less on the streets.

## 2.6  Blind spots

1. **White and middle-class.** **bell hooks** (*Ain't I a Woman*, ==1981==) and **Audre Lorde** criticised the movement for treating the problems of white, educated women as every woman's. Friedan's "trapped housewife" was a luxury for women who had always had to work.
2. **Heterosexual bias.** Friedan once called lesbian activists a "lavender menace" (==1969==); lesbian feminists pushed back.
3. **Western focus.** Women of the global South had other priorities — food, land, water, anti-colonial struggle. At the UN conference in Mexico City (==1975==) these differences came into the open (Chapter 6).
4. **Backlash.** **Phyllis Schlafly** led the campaign that defeated the **Equal Rights Amendment**; **Susan Faludi** (*Backlash*, ==1991==) described a counter-attack in the 1980s.

## 2.7  How the second wave affected women's lives

- Women entered universities and professions in large numbers.
- Discrimination at work became unlawful, and pay gaps narrowed (though they did not close).
- Women gained control over when to have children.
- Domestic violence, rape and harassment became crimes to be prosecuted.
- The division of housework was questioned — though women still do more of it everywhere.

::: pakistan | The second wave and Pakistan
The second wave reached Pakistan as the **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==). WAF's fight against the **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==) — laws that exposed rape victims to charges of adultery — was a second-wave struggle over the **body, sexuality and violence**. So was the later work on **domestic violence**, **harassment** (Act of ==2010==) and **honour crimes** (==2016==). Its secular, middle-class leadership also shared the second wave's blind spot: difficulty reaching rural and working-class women (Chapter 9).
:::
